Almost all mountains in the list are located in the [[Himalaya]] and [[Karakoram]] ranges to the south and west of the Tibetan Plateau. All peaks {{Convert|7000|m|ft|-0|abbr=on}} or higher are located in [[East Asia|East]], [[Central Asia|Central]] or [[South Asia]] in a rectangle edged by [[Noshaq]] ({{Convert|7492|m|ft|0|abbr=on|disp=or}}) on the [[Durand Line|Afghanistan–Pakistan border]] in the west, [[Jengish Chokusu]] (Tuōmù'ěr Fēng, {{Convert|7439|m|ft|0|abbr=on|disp=or}}) on the [[Kyrgyzstan]]–[[Xinjiang]] border to the north, [[Gongga Shan]] (Minya Konka, {{Convert|7556|m|ft|0|abbr=on|disp=or}}) in [[Sichuan]] to the east, and [[Kabru]] ({{Convert|7412|m|ft|0|abbr=on|disp=or}}) on the [[Sikkim]]–[[Nepal]] border to the south.

{{As of|2025|November}}, the highest peaks on four of the mountains — [[Gangkhar Puensum]], [[Labuche Kang III/East|Labuche Kang III]], [[Tongshanjiabu]] and [[Apsarasas Kangri]],{{efn|name=Apsarasas}} all located in [[India]], [[Bhutan]] or [[China]]<!--- disputes not important, still one of these two countries ---> — have not been ascended. The most recent peak to have its first ever ascent is [[Karjiang]], in China, on 13 August 2024.

The highest mountain [[Seven Summits|outside of Asia]] is [[Aconcagua]] ({{Convert|6962|m|ft|0|abbr=on|disp=or}}), the 189th highest in the world.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mount Aconcagua |date=13 July 2021 |url=https://www.worldatlas.com/mountains/mount-aconcagua.html |publisher=worldatlas.org |access-date=August 17, 2021}}</ref>
